title: The <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span> joins the <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span>
subtitle: Staal consolidates further.
view-if: chancellor = "Staal" and (unionist_in_gov or in_staal_majority_coalition) and unionist_in_gov_time >= 10 and ((unionist_leader == "Hughes" and not unionist_gnu and bracht_time >= 8) or republicans_staal >= 8 and unionist_leader == "Sedgewick" or republicans_staal >= 10) and gnu_formed
new-page: true
priority: 1
tags: event
max-visits: 1

= <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span> joins the <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span>

The <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span>—the great confessional bloc of the Faithful, the one pillar of the old order Staal courted longest and won last—has agreed to fold itself into the <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span>. It is the keystone he wanted above all the others: a party of order, property, and the Faithful, lending the front a respectability no rally of his own could ever buy.

For us, it is the worst of the news. The <span style="color: #000000;">Unionists</span> were the last great bloc that might have stood beside the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> against Staal in a real crisis—the swing weight of the whole centre. With them inside the tent, the broad middle the chancellor has been assembling is very nearly the whole of the Commonwealth that matters; what remains outside it is ourselves, the banned <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span>, and the irreconcilable <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span> who think Staal too soft. The end, if not near, is no longer far.

- @schleicher_2: The <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">Commonwealth National Unity Front</span> welcomes its newest member.

@schleicher_2
on-arrival: {!
Q.unionist_gnu = 1;

Q.pro_republic -= Q.unionist_votes;

Q.unionist_relation = (Q.gnu_relation - 10);

Q.gnu_r += Q.unionist_r;
Q.unionist_r = 0;
Q.bvp_r = 0;
!}

